\paragraph{What we find.}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em,itemsep=0pt,topsep=2pt]
\item \textbf{Regime map.} Regression beats DM in most cells; the spillover share above which it wins, $s^\star$, rises with degree (about $0.1$, $0.19$, $0.38$ at $d=5,20,80$, $\sigma=0.5$) because the variance does. A threshold response is the exception on synthetic graphs, not on the two real ones (\S\ref{sec:regime}).
\item \textbf{Variance and bias differ.} Variance follows leverage (SD exponents $-0.50$ in $n$, $+0.55$ in $1{+}d$ where the specification is correct). Bias is predicted within Monte-Carlo error in 87\% of cells by a formula that uses the true response, and depends on degree differently for each mechanism (\S\ref{sec:varbias}).
\item \textbf{Two negative results.} No data-computable score we tried predicts the bias usefully, and the usual 95\% interval covers 74\% of Bernoulli cells, failing where bias exceeds noise, not where leverage is high (\S\ref{sec:diag}, \S\ref{sec:coverage}).
\item \textbf{Designs and learners.} Cluster designs trade bias against variance; on a sample of cells, flexible learners do not beat the regression except on the threshold response (\S\ref{sec:cluster}, \S\ref{sec:regime}).
\end{itemize}

